WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.635
Some things just
don't go together--

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:07.006
oil and water, gas and
matches, tequila and fireworks.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:08.775
So you can imagine
my concern when

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.644
I heard about a combination
of kayaking and surfing.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.781
[water waves]

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.683
Then I saw this
and thought perhaps

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.119
I'm worrying about nothing.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:24.924
And then I saw this and realized
I was right the first time.

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:28.795
All right.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.097
If we're going to go treating
a kayak like a surfboard,

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:36.202
we'd better prep on center of
buoyancy and hydrodynamic drag.

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.972
To remain stable, a kayaker
keeps the center of mass

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:42.275
directly over the kayak
center of buoyancy,

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:46.579
which is in the middle
of its submerged volume.

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.649
Traveling out to sea, it helps
to keep the kayak perpendicular

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.485
to the waves, because
turning sideways

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:56.489
can mean more hydrodynamic
drag and a salty roll.

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:01.961
When catching a wave, you must
ensure the nose doesn't dig

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.397
into the water,
otherwise the resulting

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:08.401
increase in hydrodynamic drag
could produce a turning effect.

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:13.807
Waves can travel
hundreds of miles,

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.743
relentlessly building speed
and power before smashing

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.646
into your kayak, but we
should be OK-- providing

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:20.480
we remember the rules.

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:22.482
[noise]

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.951
Hold on a minute, should we
be perpendicular to the wave

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.286
or parallel to it?

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.489
[music playing]

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:35.662
Yes, it was perpendicular.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.564
By hitting the wave
almost parallel

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.266
he presents a
large surface area,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.302
which leads to enough
hydrodynamic drag

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.102
to flip the kayak.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:46.706
[music playing]

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.744
We are out and ready
to catch a wave.

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:55.148
I'm videoing this just in
case things don't go well.

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.383
That's a little negative.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.551
Confident, confident.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:01.554
[noise]

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.992
But well-founded.

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:10.430
Good start, but here the
nose digs in slows down

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:16.269
and our friends enjoy the
benefits of the turning effect.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:22.175
Now these guys have got
it nice and perpendicular,

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.142
minimal drag.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.414
Like a knife through
salty butter.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.650
[music playing]

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.753
NARRATOR: Maybe it's safer
to avoid all that nasty drag

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.690
and head out of the
surf to calm open sea.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.058
There it is.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.193
There it is.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.161
NARRATOR: Oh, look-- a
delightful little fish.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.229
[music playing]

